   "subject": "Re: multiple geom_line",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I think the reason you appear to get three graphs is because the two\r<br>ETS series nearly overlap each other. The data is certainly there:\r<br>\r<br>&gt; with(aut, table(group, data))\r<br>          data\r<br>group      *2000 *2005\r<br>  c arfima    15    15\r<br>  c ets       15    15\r<br>  M arfima    15    15\r<br>  M ets       15    15\r<br>\r<br>and ggplot2 registered no complaints. In addition, the legend has all\r<br>four levels of group, so it recognized the data. If you look at the\r<br>two ets series, though, you&#39;ll see that they almost completely overlap\r<br>- the differences between them are tiny. The only hint you can see in\r<br>the graph is a little tinge of gray-green in the upper right corner of\r<br>the 2000 panel colored in purple for M ets. Perhaps a better way to\r<br>see this is to modify the graph:\r<br>\r<br>ggplot(aut, aes(x = Year, y = TFR, color = method)) +\r<br>    geom_line(size = 1) +\r<br>    facet_grid(pcd ~ data)\r<br>\r<br>where it is easier to see that the ets graphs in each column are\r<br>almost identical.\r<br>\r<br>Dennis\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
